#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""fetch-intel — collect the primary-source data this repository cites.

Writes JSON under data/<YYYY-MM-DD>/ so that every number in the docs can be
re-derived instead of trusted. Python 3 standard library only: no requests, no
bs4, no jq. Network is the only external dependency.

  tools/intel/fetch-intel                       # today's date, current quarter
  tools/intel/fetch-intel --date 2026-10-22 --cpu Oct2026
  tools/intel/fetch-intel --sources oracle,kev  # subset
  tools/intel/fetch-intel --recent-hours 12     # broad NVD + GHSA window
  tools/intel/fetch-intel --x-posts 2113538808126038500
  tools/intel/fetch-intel --sources php,kev            # PHP's own state
  tools/intel/fetch-intel --out /tmp/intel      # somewhere else

Exit codes: 0 = every source fetched, 10 = partial (some source failed, the rest
was written), 1 = usage error, 2 = nothing could be fetched.

Why this exists: when an advisory is republished, the only way to notice is to
diff the source. Keeping the raw shape of each source (not a hand-copied table)
is what makes that diff trustworthy.
"""

